# The forest beetle's body (#327, D101): the `dead` frame of beetle.grid, pixel for pixel, in a decor-tier palette (D76): a
# dim colour with a contour in its own hue, no ink 0b0814, no moon rim, so a harmless body never reads as a live beetle. The game
# draws the active `dead` frame at the kill and cross-fades into this one; it rocks when the hero passes (reactive, not idle).
# Forest recolour (#236, D108, 2026-11-20): hand-set tone roles (contour k, shade d, shell t, lit s, glint h, legs n/L) on one
# dusky indigo (OKLab L 0.176 to 0.242, chroma 0.05, hue -75). It lies on forest-1's near-black ground and forest-2's blue sky,
# and a cool hue sits between both (D108 band 0.08..0.20 at every spot where a beetle can die); no ink 0b0814, no moon rim.
# Keep it in step with beetle.grid.
# Forest-2 conversion (#236, D112): the dusky indigo matched forest-2's grove spruces (body 0.034); the tone roles now sit on the
# spider's and snail's dried umber (hue 1.0 rad, chroma 0.045, L kept): body 0.094 to 0.175 at every spot, chips in BeetleFx follow.
size 24 16
color k 1f0a00
color d 240f01
color t 271202
color s 2b1605
color h 2f1a08
color n 210d00
color L 2a1404
frame body
